course: dictionary
lesson: 9728
title: Telling the time with shadows
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sundial is an old instrument that shows the time of day without any moving hands. «A sundial tells the time from the position of a shadow.» So instrument means a tool that does a job, here showing the time.
explain: The most important part is the gnomon, the pointer or stick. «A sundial tells the time by the shadow of a stick called a gnomon, which moves round as the Earth turns.» The gnomon throws its shadow onto the marks, and the shadow shows the hour.
explain: Use the clues to check. «A sundial does not work on a cloudy day or at night, because there is no shadow.» No Sun means no shadow, so no time can be read. Try your meaning in the sentence: a sundial that tells the time at midnight would not make sense, so the Sun must be needed.
fact: A sundial is an old instrument that shows the time of day.
fact: The gnomon is the pointer, and it casts a shadow onto a flat dial marked with hours.
fact: A sundial only works when the Sun shines, because it needs a shadow.
fact: «Shadows are long» when the Sun is low in the morning and evening, and «short» when it is high at midday.
fact: As the Earth turns, the shadow moves round the dial, so the sundial shows a different hour.
exercise: What part of a sundial casts the shadow? | The little hands | The glass cover | The numbers on the face | *The gnomon, a pointer
exercise: «A sundial does not work on a cloudy day or at night.» Why not? | The gnomon is made of metal | The marks have worn away | *There is no shadow to show the time | The dial is too heavy to move
exercise: «Shadows are long when the Sun is low in the morning and evening.» When are shadows short? | When it is dark at night | When the Sun is low in the morning | When the Sun is low in the evening | *When the Sun is high at midday
test: «Shadows are long when the Sun is low in the morning and evening and short when it is high at midday.» Where is the Sun at midday? | *High in the sky | Behind the clouds | Low in the sky | Under the ground
test: Pupils make their own sundial with a stick and chalk marks. What does their sundial need to work? | *Sunshine, to make a shadow on the chalk marks | Warm water | A battery | Nothing, it works in the dark
exam: What does sundial mean? | A lamp that lights up the garden | A dial with numbers that glows at night | *An old instrument that shows the time from the shadow of a pointer on a marked dial | A tool for measuring rain
